{
  "configuration_id": "config_045",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_006 (scvi_n200, resnet50, avgpool, weighted) achieved 0.678 acc at 5.90 GPU hrs, clearly outperforming config_008 (pca_n2000, same other settings) at 0.639 acc and 10.60 GPU hrs. This establishes scvi_n200 as the preferred RNA encoder. The biggest remaining uncertainty is whether the image encoder is a bottleneck: ResNet50 (~25M params) is substantially smaller than ImageNet-pretrained ViT-L (~300M params), and the image branch may be limiting the multimodal model's ceiling. Question tested: config_045 is a clean single-variable change from the current best (resnet50 → imagenet_vit_l, all else identical: scvi_n200, multiprofile, avgpool, weighted), isolating the effect of a stronger visual encoder. Why this is the best next experiment: with 7 experiments remaining, identifying whether a stronger image encoder yields a meaningful accuracy gain is the highest-information single test—it determines whether subsequent experiments should invest in ViT-L-based combinations (e.g., ViT-L + transformer aggregation) or stay with the cheaper ResNet50 and focus on other levers (aggregation, fusion). Expected relative cost: moderate-to-high, likely ~12–16 GPU hrs (ViT-L forward/backward is roughly 2–3× ResNet50), but justified because a strong image encoder is the most probable source of a several-point accuracy jump and the information gained will shape the remaining 6 experiments."
}

